1
00:00:24,010 --> 00:00:29,617
Hello and welcome to the NPTEL MOOCs course on Foundation for Virtual and Augmented Reality Systems.

2
00:00:30,798 --> 00:00:39,689
We are going to conclude our discussion on resource optimization, particularly VR resource optimization, with this part of the lecture.

3
00:00:40,470 --> 00:00:52,785
So, we have talked about the basic idea of computer graphics, then the 3D pipeline, the computer graphics pipeline that we can

4
00:00:53,221 --> 00:01:03,278
envision to render a static image on a screen, just to demonstrate the computational complexities involved in the rendering process and

5
00:01:03,278 --> 00:01:14,301
the stages of the pipeline, also how the pipeline can be implemented in software through software libraries and in hardware using GPU and graphics card.

6
00:01:16,475 --> 00:01:23,183
So, in this lecture, let us try to see how this knowledge can help us make decisions while building practical systems.

7
00:01:26,587 --> 00:01:34,977
So, in order to understand how we can use this idea in practice, let us revisit our Majuli case study, which we have discussed earlier.

8
00:01:36,099 --> 00:01:46,431
Just to recap, this is a river island on the river Brahmaputra, which is in the eastern part of India, as shown here

9
00:01:47,002 --> 00:01:47,483
on the map.

10
00:01:49,024 --> 00:02:03,299
The island is here in India and is surrounded by the Brahmaputra and its tributary. It is the world's largest inhabited river island.

11
00:02:04,320 --> 00:02:13,330
Our objective is to create a virtual tour of the river island so that the visitor in the virtual tour can

12
00:02:14,018 --> 00:02:25,034
perceive or can experience the ecological and cultural, both tangible and intangible, cultural heritage of the island.

13
00:02:26,016 --> 00:02:32,585
Now, earlier we talked about the different points of interest on the island.

14
00:02:32,605 --> 00:02:34,648
These are the information points on the island.

15
00:02:35,549 --> 00:02:42,760
So, a large number of points are there, which include Shatras or Vaishnavite monasteries, villages,

16
00:02:43,448 --> 00:02:48,135
Tribal villages, and there are towns and other places of interest.

17
00:02:50,419 --> 00:02:55,606
So, a large number of points, each point is having a geographical spread.

18
00:02:56,107 --> 00:03:00,714
So, there are a large number of points of interest in each point as well.

19
00:03:01,235 --> 00:03:12,612
So, two levels of vast information: one is the whole island; the other one is each information point in the island also contains a large amount of

20
00:03:13,469 --> 00:03:15,312
data of interest.

21
00:03:16,694 --> 00:03:26,469
So, we want an interactive virtual tour, a virtual reality tour of the island, an immersive experience, which is a combination of exploration techniques.

22
00:03:28,833 --> 00:03:39,008
Because the geographical spread is large, single techniques such as walking may not be good, or only teleportation may not be good to give a good feeling.

23
00:03:39,028 --> 00:03:42,193
So, we require a combination of exploration techniques.

24
00:03:43,473 --> 00:03:48,763
Now, what we did was to use a walking-based technique,

25
00:03:48,763 --> 00:04:01,560
a navigation technique at each information point, and a teleportation technique or vehicular movement technique, a navigation technique across points on the island.

26
00:04:01,920 --> 00:04:11,134
So, within a point we use walking for navigation; between points we use teleportation or travel through a vehicle metaphor.

27
00:04:14,337 --> 00:04:18,942
So, these are the combination of navigation techniques we use to explore the whole island.

28
00:04:22,107 --> 00:04:29,370
And at each spot, the objective is that it should reveal all related information, including the

29
00:04:29,370 --> 00:04:41,391
heritage information, and let the user use such information interactively, and the user can interactively explore the place.

30
00:04:44,172 --> 00:04:56,937
So, in order to achieve the objective, we have collected a multimodal archive of information, which included regular and 360-degree images plus videos to

31
00:04:56,937 --> 00:05:04,957
capture indigenous architectural heritage of Satras and village life, 3D models for landscape and architecture,

32
00:05:04,957 --> 00:05:13,639
2D drone shots to showcase landscape including flora and fauna because ecological heritage also we wanted to capture.

33
00:05:14,648 --> 00:05:21,839
then 2D videos to depict cultural or intangible heritage plus textual description.

34
00:05:22,500 --> 00:05:31,113
So, all this information together constituted the whole application because the amount of information is vast.

35
00:05:31,593 --> 00:05:36,340
So, the application size also became quite large, almost 10 gigabytes.

36
00:05:37,482 --> 00:05:42,790
Now, the challenge was to render the content and allow the users to interact.

37
00:05:42,810 --> 00:05:44,132
Now, as we have already seen,

38
00:05:44,483 --> 00:06:00,153
Rendering such vast content involves lots of computation because if you note here, we have multimodal content rich in details, and the implication of such content is that

39
00:06:00,153 --> 00:06:09,883
we require significant computational support to render it efficiently in real time so that the immersive experience is realistic.

40
00:06:11,382 --> 00:06:20,310
So, we require fast storage and retrieval support as well as high computational support because the rendering process will be complex.

41
00:06:21,171 --> 00:06:27,670
Earlier we talked about rendering simple static images, which itself was computationally intensive, and here

42
00:06:27,670 --> 00:06:34,603
we are having rich multimodal content which can be interactively changed and includes videos and animations.

43
00:06:35,804 --> 00:06:38,807
So, the computational requirement is many times higher.

44
00:06:42,382 --> 00:06:53,576
Our regular systems cannot support such an application having almost 10 GB of content, rich multimodal realistic content.

45
00:06:53,596 --> 00:06:56,379
So, we had to go for some optimization technique.

46
00:06:56,399 --> 00:06:58,602
So, let us talk about two such techniques.

47
00:06:58,622 --> 00:07:06,432
Optimization 1: here we wanted to implement our system for the Oculus Quest 2 application.

48
00:07:07,475 --> 00:07:14,466
The device, as shown here, comprises a headset and handheld controllers.

49
00:07:15,948 --> 00:07:20,956
Now, the Oculus Quest 2 is a standalone computing device having its own processor and memory.

50
00:07:21,437 --> 00:07:24,381
We do not need to tether it with any computer.

51
00:07:26,144 --> 00:07:28,648
No need to tether the headset to a fixed computer.

52
00:07:29,369 --> 00:07:34,056
But the limitation is that its computing power is

53
00:07:34,458 --> 00:07:38,883
less as compared to a workstation.

54
00:07:38,903 --> 00:07:47,573
And storage is also relatively less compared to a workstation, a high-end graphics workstation.

55
00:07:47,593 --> 00:07:58,927
So, then we had to go for some optimization strategy to reduce the size of the application and also associated computations, including memory access.

56
00:07:58,967 --> 00:08:04,313
So, if we reduce the size, then the requirement for rendering

57
00:08:04,867 --> 00:08:14,322
will be reduced, which also involves the requirement for accessing data from memory for rendering.

58
00:08:14,342 --> 00:08:15,584
Now, what strategy did we use?

59
00:08:16,465 --> 00:08:19,189
We used a cloud computing-based approach.

60
00:08:19,850 --> 00:08:32,089
So, we use the cloud to store all the videos, not the images, but the videos, because videos take up more space.

61
00:08:33,823 --> 00:08:42,755
When the user selects a video thumbnail from the video player in the Quest, that is the video player interface provided within the immersive environment,

62
00:08:42,755 --> 00:08:53,113
as we have discussed earlier in the implementation part of the lecture, the corresponding video is loaded from the cloud.

63
00:08:53,173 --> 00:08:59,663
So, the video is not stored locally, so the storage requirement is less, and the accessing requirement is

64
00:09:00,082 --> 00:09:03,726
less. Accessing operations are also computationally intensive.

65
00:09:04,287 --> 00:09:06,089
So, the fewer the operations, the better.

66
00:09:07,832 --> 00:09:12,037
So, this led to a reduced application size of 5.1 GB.

67
00:09:12,077 --> 00:09:16,662
So, because some parts, the videos, are stored in the cloud.

68
00:09:16,682 --> 00:09:28,637
So, the application deployed on the client device, that is the Oculus Quest 2, is reduced to 5.1 GB, and a corresponding computational

69
00:09:29,393 --> 00:09:30,735
reduction happened.

70
00:09:31,416 --> 00:09:45,613
However, because we are relying on a cloud server, cloud services, there were additional concerns apart from rendering, that is particularly related to network connectivity.

71
00:09:47,436 --> 00:09:56,667
In order to load the high-quality videos on a real-time basis, we required high bandwidth support and also network coverage.

72
00:09:58,165 --> 00:09:59,446
Latency should be minimal.

73
00:09:59,787 --> 00:10:06,334
So, if these issues are not resolved, then cloud-based optimization would not be of much help.

74
00:10:06,815 --> 00:10:20,950
However, this is just to show that to take care of optimization concerns in rendering, we can recourse to different technologies so that rendering optimization can take place.

75
00:10:22,792 --> 00:10:26,316
That may lead to other concerns which we also need to address.

76
00:10:28,912 --> 00:10:31,856
Now, let us talk about another optimization.

77
00:10:33,078 --> 00:10:46,678
We also wanted to implement for everyday smartphones; that is, we wanted to deploy it on a smartphone and put it in a cardboard for an immersive experience, as illustrated in this figure.

78
00:10:46,738 --> 00:10:55,932
So, we have this cardboard type of system within which we can put our smartphone and

79
00:10:56,755 --> 00:11:10,730
if we create stereoscopic views, then when we wear it as a headset, we get to see an immersive environment without using any expensive headsets.

80
00:11:11,951 --> 00:11:21,642
Cardboards are very cheap, only about 500 to 1000 rupees, Indian rupees.

81
00:11:21,662 --> 00:11:25,446
And everybody has smartphones, so if we deploy, then we can have

82
00:11:26,624 --> 00:11:35,132
a ready-made immersive experience without spending too much money on the appropriate device.

83
00:11:36,334 --> 00:11:47,018
However, in order to do that, we had to go for some optimization strategy because the size is more than what typically smartphones support and the

84
00:11:47,018 --> 00:11:55,112
associated computation also; typically, smartphones do not support such a high amount of computation, average smartphones.

85
00:11:57,404 --> 00:12:00,228
So, we had to go for some other optimization strategy.

86
00:12:02,731 --> 00:12:07,537
In this case, what we did is we did compression.

87
00:12:07,918 --> 00:12:18,151
We compressed the images that we have to reduce the storage requirements, and we could not utilize the videos, most of the videos.

88
00:12:18,231 --> 00:12:21,756
So, video playback support was removed.

89
00:12:24,219 --> 00:12:27,023
So, together what they resulted in is

90
00:12:27,998 --> 00:12:35,486
a kind of less realistic experience as compared to a full version implementation.

91
00:12:35,506 --> 00:12:42,653
But with these optimizations, we could reduce the overall size to 0.7 GB from roughly 10 GB.

92
00:12:43,233 --> 00:12:44,755
As you can see, it is much less.

93
00:12:48,018 --> 00:12:52,843
We could have also explored cloud-based support as well, cloud-based solutions as well.

94
00:12:54,465 --> 00:12:56,807
So, in that case, video playback we could have kept.

95
00:12:58,508 --> 00:13:04,595
However, you can try it at your end for large applications.

96
00:13:05,316 --> 00:13:14,187
So, the idea here is just to show that in order to take care of computational issues associated with rendering immersive experiences,

97
00:13:14,187 --> 00:13:23,757
we can take recourse to different approaches such as taking cloud-based services or going for compression techniques.

98
00:13:24,901 --> 00:13:30,006
And in extreme cases, we may have to remove some features.

99
00:13:31,988 --> 00:13:43,499
So that is what we can do in practice because we know that if we try to deploy the whole system for any platform, then it is not possible.

100
00:13:44,180 --> 00:13:45,281
It will lead to problems.

101
00:13:45,441 --> 00:13:47,043
And why will the problems come?

102
00:13:47,123 --> 00:13:50,146
Now we have a foundation to understand that.

103
00:13:50,166 --> 00:13:53,389
That is because rendering will not be possible.

104
00:13:53,723 --> 00:13:54,805
with any device.

105
00:13:56,989 --> 00:13:58,852
So, let us summarize what we have learned.

106
00:13:59,694 --> 00:14:03,541
So, we have talked about the idea of resource optimization in virtual reality.

107
00:14:05,224 --> 00:14:18,347
In order to understand that, we introduced computer graphics, basically the pipeline stages, the 3D graphics pipeline, its stages and its implementation in software as well as in hardware.

108
00:14:19,913 --> 00:14:25,300
where we explained what a GPU is, how it is organized, and how it is used to implement the stages.

109
00:14:27,422 --> 00:14:30,787
And also we talked about how such knowledge is useful in practice.

110
00:14:32,689 --> 00:14:38,196
So, in this entire lecture on immersive experience, we referred to several concepts.

111
00:14:38,416 --> 00:14:45,685
We did not go into the details of those, particularly related to computer graphics, as the objective was to keep it simple.

112
00:14:46,441 --> 00:14:48,844
bare minimum things for your understanding.

113
00:14:49,325 --> 00:14:59,137
However, you can refer to this book, Computer Graphics by Oxford University Press, written by myself to know more details about the topics covered.

114
00:14:59,717 --> 00:15:01,780
All the topics I have taken from that book only.

115
00:15:02,000 --> 00:15:13,475
Also, there is one NPTEL MOOC course offered by me on Computer Graphics, which again covers all the topics mentioned here.

116
00:15:13,855 --> 00:15:14,676
So, you can also

117
00:15:15,398 --> 00:15:23,266
go through the modules of that course to know in more detail about the stages.

118
00:15:26,249 --> 00:15:37,001
So, I hope you could follow the overall idea of the resource optimization concept and why computer graphics is relevant and how

119
00:15:37,001 --> 00:15:43,127
we can understand the rendering process complexities and how that knowledge can help us take decisions.

120
00:15:43,833 --> 00:15:54,332
in deploying systems. So, looking forward to meeting you in the next lecture. Thank you, goodbye, and happy learning.

